    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a deck project in vancouver and need to double check some math. we're using western red cedar for the framing. can you explain the difference between live load and dead load calculations for the deck structure and how they factor into the beam sizing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Dead load refers to the permanent weight of the structure itself, including the cedar framing and decking materials. Live load accounts for temporary, moveable loads like people, furniture, or snow accumulation on the deck. When sizing beams, you must calculate the total anticipated load (Dead Load + Live Load) to ensure the structural members can safely support the combined weight according to building codes. This combined load dictates the necessary beam size and reinforcement to prevent excessive deflection or failure.",
      "source": "target",
