    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. moisture and hardware make sense. i'm mostly worried about the hidden stuff though, like if there are small defects in the timber or mistakes in the joist layout that don't show up right away but end up causing problems down the line. are there typical \"invisible\" risks or weird little things that tend to mess up a deck's integrity over time?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Invisible risks often involve inadequate connection detailing, such as hanger failure or undersized ledger board connections to the house structure. Improperly spaced joists or framing members can create localized stress points that lead to premature failure, especially under concentrated loads. Another subtle issue is wood movement due to fluctuating humidity, which can cause fasteners to loosen or racking to occur if the design lacks sufficient bracing. Regularly checking load paths and connection integrity during construction is key to preventing these hidden problems.",
      "source": "target",
